CloudBurrow patch 2 of 2 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1086).

The bug: every request finds its project (withProjectMiddleware,
Repository.FindProject), and the emulator read the project with every job
it had run, each job's metadata and its whole result, every row, decoded
from JSON (findProjects, findJobs); and each new job wrote the project's
list of job IDs again (AddJob, UpdateProject). So every request, whatever it
was, got slower with each job kept. Measured on the emulator alone, on an
Apple silicon Mac: SELECT 1 through jobs.query took 0.006 s on a fresh
emulator, 0.14 s after 20 queries of 50,000 rows and 0.27 s after 40, and
datasets.list the same; after 1,000 queries of 10 rows each, 0.37 s. Nothing
ever dropped a result.

The patch reads a project with no jobs, and a job when a handler asks for it
(Project.Job, Project.Jobs, now given a context and returning an error),
with its metadata and not its result, which only Wait (jobs.getQueryResults)
reads; the order jobs were added in, which the project's row listed, is in
a table of its own, cloudburrow_job_order, which a database made before the
patch is moved to. And a query's result rows are kept as BigQuery keeps a
query's temporary result table, up to 24 hours, and while the results kept
come to at most 256 MiB of JSON, the oldest dropped first at the next job:
jobs.getQueryResults of a job whose result was dropped answers 404 notFound,
and the job itself is kept. results_test.go fails without the patch
(TestFindProjectReadsNoJobResults: FindProject decoded a job's result) and
passes with it.

